{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788354761,"nonce":"eau/0qvsr+hpcxz3MRR6esbHDow=","message":"**PARKED, worker, repair round 2026-09-02 (branch `fix/a1644d6-enclosure`).** No\ncode shipped for this issue. The Notes section's park condition is met, but not\nfor the reason it states — the durable handle DOES exist, and the design\nquestion sits one step further in than the issue puts it.\n\n\nA regression detector serving both acceptance items off one mechanism:\nobserve each room once, bank `{handle → role, role-defining contents}`, and on a\nlater read report any handle whose role changed or whose defining building is\ngone, naming old role, new role and the tick. It would have hung off the same\nroll-up `a1644d6` just added (`rooms.layouts_unenclosed` /\n`digest.construction`), and shared `LayoutEnclosureWatch`'s in-memory tracking.\n\n\nRead against the decompiled source by member name.\n\n**The issue's stated blocker is half right and the correction matters.**\n`Map.MapUpdate` does call `regionAndRoomUpdater.TryRebuildDirtyRegionsAndRooms()`\nevery frame (`Verse/Map.cs`), but that method returns immediately on\n`!map.regionDirtyer.AnyDirty`, and when it does run,\n`RegionAndRoomUpdater.CreateOrAttachToExistingRooms` **reuses** the old `Room`\nobject wherever `FindCurrentRoomNeighborWithMostRegions` finds one — only the\n`room == null` branch reaches `Room.MakeNew`, which is the sole site that takes\na fresh `nextRoomID`. So `Room.ID` is *stable across ordinary play*. A naive\nper-frame diff would not, in fact, report churn most of the time.\n\n**What actually kills `Room.ID` is a LOAD, and that is decisive.**\n`TryRebuildDirtyRegionsAndRooms` calls `RebuildAllRegionsAndRooms()` when\n`!initialized`, which sets every cell dirty and rebuilds every room from\nscratch, so every room takes a new `ID`. `nextRoomID` is a plain static and is\nnot scribed. Run `m1-20260901` spanned sessions and bench relaunches, and\n`664e9b9` explicitly allows that — so a `Room.ID`-keyed baseline is blind\nexactly across the boundary a fire-and-reload run most needs it. That is\nsharper than \"reassigned every frame\" and it is the fact that rules the ID out.\n\n**A durable handle exists and is already shipped.** The issue lists three\ncandidates; the third is right and it is already in the mod.\n`Spatial.cs`'s `LandmarkComponent` is a `GameComponent` **with** `ExposeData`\n(`Scribe_Collections.Look(ref landmarks, \"autoRimmerLandmarks\", …)`) that\npersists named cells in the save, `room-at` already resolves cell → room, and\n`templates/freezer-kitchen.md` already instructs the agent to register one after\nplacement. `landmark {set:{name:\"freezer\", at:P}}` is a durable, scribed,\nplayer-named room handle today. The other two candidates fail: a layout id is\nsession-scoped (`Layouts.Clear()` at every game boundary) and only exists for\nrooms placed by `place-layout` — run `m1-20260901`'s barracks was\ncolonist-built and had none — and the role-defining building's\n`thingIDNumber`, while genuinely scribed, is the thing that gets destroyed, so\nit cannot be the handle you notice its absence *from*.\n\n**So the open question is not the handle. It is the BASELINE.** \"This room used\nto be a Barracks\" is a fact about the past, and nothing in this mod remembers\nthe past across a load. `AgentGameComponent` has no `ExposeData` — grep it — and\nthis project deliberately does not buy durability with new scribed state\n(`d16a463` is open on exactly that, and `f9dadc7`'s resolution says so in\nterms). That is a decision above a worker's pay grade, and it is the whole\nissue.\n\n**And one finding splits the issue in two.** Acceptance item 2 — a destroyed\nplayer building of consequence is reported — **needs no room identity at all**.\n`JournalHooks.cs` already carries five Harmony postfixes including\n`Frame.CompleteConstruction` and `Frame.FailConstruction`; a destruction hook is\nthe same shape, emits a journal row plus a digest roll-up, and answers item 2\ncompletely with zero persistence. It is item 1 (a room's *role* changed) that\nneeds a baseline, and only item 1.\n\n\n1. **In-memory baseline keyed on a landmark cell.** Follows `f9dadc7`'s settled\n   pattern verbatim — track what this process has seen, publish `tracked_since`,\n   and distinguish *regressed* / *not regressed* / *not known yet*. Cost: blind\n   across every load, which is the case that matters most (a fire, a reload, a\n   session boundary). Cheap, honest, partial. **The report would be silent\n   exactly when the run it exists for spans sessions.**\n2. **Scribe the baseline in a new `ExposeData`.** Complete and durable. Cost:\n   precisely the hazard `d16a463` is open on — the observation surface writing\n   scribed state — plus a save-compatibility surface the mod does not have\n   today. Not a trade this round should make, per `f9dadc7`.\n3. **Extend the already-scribed `LandmarkComponent` to remember the role\n   observed when the landmark was set.** Much smaller than (2): the component is\n   already scribed, already player-driven, and the mod would be recording a fact\n   the *agent asserted* (\"this cell is the freezer\") rather than silently\n   snapshotting the map. Cost: still new scribed state, still `d16a463`'s\n   family, and it only covers rooms somebody bothered to name. **This is the\n   option worth putting to Evan**, and it is the only one that answers item 1\n   across a reload.\n4. **Split the issue: ship item 2 as an EVENT, park item 1.** A Harmony postfix\n   on building destruction — journal row + digest roll-up, \"of consequence\"\n   filtered on `def.building.isEdifice || bed || workbench || cooler/heater ||\n   door`, or simply on player faction plus a wealth floor. No identity, no\n   persistence, no baseline. Cost: the \"of consequence\" filter is a judgement\n   call that will need one round of tuning, and item 1 stays open. Closes the\n   half of this issue that a fire actually trips.\n\n\nSplit it, per option 4 + option 3. Item 2 is shippable now and is what\n`checklists/triggered.md`'s fire path needs. Item 1 wants a ruling from Evan on\noption 3 before anyone writes it, because the alternative — option 1 — is a\ndetector that goes quiet across the exact boundary the fire crossed, and a\nregression report you cannot trust after a reload is the cry-wolf failure with\nthe sign flipped.\n\nNot filed as new issues, deliberately: the split is a change to THIS issue's\ncontract and belongs to the orchestrator, not to a worker mid-round.","files":null}]}